1. Inspector: What It Should Become

Inspector SubToolbox

Purpose: Understand the selected asset, its properties, its history, and how it connects to the rest of ViewTube.

Rather than creating a separate module for every kind of inspection, I recommend one Inspector SubToolbox with these sections:

Section	Responsibilities

Overview	Asset name, type, thumbnail or preview, status, project, and key metadata.
Metadata	Edit the title, description, tags, custom fields, and supported descriptive metadata.
Technical Details	Dimensions, resolution, aspect ratio, duration, codec, file size, and available technical properties.
Provenance	Show where the asset originated, which tool created it, and relevant generation or editing records.
Lineage & Versions	Show parent assets, derivatives, version history, and relationships between assets.
Rights & Usage	Display licensing, usage restrictions, attribution, and known usage references.
Health & Validation	Identify missing metadata, invalid properties, unsupported formats, and other actionable problems.


The Inspector should adapt to the selected asset.

For example, selecting an image should expose image dimensions and image-specific details. Selecting a video should expose video properties. Selecting a document should expose document-specific information.

Sections should only display properties that are relevant and supported by the available data.

How it should behave

Use the existing Toolbox/SubToolbox architecture.

Use the canonical UI components and styling system rather than hard-coded copies.

Integrate with the existing asset selection and workspace state.

Preserve the selected asset when switching Inspector sections.

Keep edits connected to the canonical asset record.

Show the source of information and distinguish verified metadata from unavailable information.

Provide contextual actions to open the appropriate specialist tool.

Work consistently across desktop and mobile layouts.


Important: The Inspector should inspect assets and perform appropriate lightweight metadata operations. It should not become a second video editor, Script Architect, or generation engine.


