4. How the Modules Should Work Together

The modules should not operate as isolated mini-applications.

Consider a creator selecting a video asset.

1. Asset Library displays the video and its basic information.


2. Inspector reveals its metadata, technical details, provenance, and available versions.


3. Compare & Versions helps the creator select the correct candidate.


4. Processing & Derivatives offers supported transformations.


5. Task Center tracks any asynchronous processing.


6. Project Readiness identifies whether the resulting asset satisfies project requirements.


7. Send To & Handoff routes the asset to the appropriate specialist tool.


8. The result returns to the appropriate canonical asset or project workflow.



Throughout this process, the asset should retain its identity and history.

The creator should not have to manually recreate context every time they move between modules.

Shared rules for every SubToolbox

Every module should follow the same standards:

One clear purpose: Its title should communicate what it does.

Shared primitives: Use the canonical UI components and tokens.

Consistent behavior: Module headers, controls, spacing, and expansion behavior should follow the existing system.

Context awareness: Know the selected asset, selection count, project, and available operations.

Appropriate actions: Do not display irrelevant operations for an asset type.

Persistent state: Preserve useful selections and settings according to the workspace persistence rules.

Honest status: Distinguish completed work, pending operations, unavailable functionality, and errors.

Canonical data: Use the existing asset, project, ContentBuild, and handoff systems.

Responsive layout: Modules must remain usable on mobile.

No duplicate engines: Route work to existing specialist systems.



